---
// src/pages/tmgo.astro — TM Go architecture case study
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content="TM Go architecture case study by Austin Nafe — the engineering decisions behind an AI agent trusted with real data, and its scaling path.">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Oswald:wght@500;600;700&family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:title" content="TM Go — Architecture Case Study">
  <meta property="og:description" content="Constraining an agent so it could be trusted with real data — the decisions, the reliability, and how it scales.">
  <meta property="og:image" content="/profile.jpg">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  <title>TM Go — Architecture Case Study · Austin Nafe</title>
  <style is:global>
  :root {
    /* TM GO brand — the app's "cream" logo theme: cream paper, navy ink, orange accent */
    --bg: #f5efe4;
    --alt: #efe8d8;
    --card: #fffdf8;
    --ink: #1a2540;
    --text: #3d4a66;
    --muted: #5b6577;
    --faint: #8b95a8;
    --line: #e0d7c2;
    --line-soft: #e8e0cd;
    --accent: #ff7a3d;
    --accent-2: #e5622a;
    --accent-tint: #ffe8de;
    --red: #dc2626;
    --red-tint: #fee2e2;
    --green: #16a34a;
    --green-tint: #dcfce7;
    --amber-ink: #8a6300;
    --amber-tint: #fef3c7;
    --shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(26,37,64,.05), 0 6px 20px rgba(26,37,64,.06);
    --head: 'Oswald', sans-serif;
    --sans: 'DM Sans', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif;
    --mono: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    --rail-w: 264px; --maxw: 820px;
  }
  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
  html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
  body {
    background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.62; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; letter-spacing: -0.002em;
  }
  ::selection { background: #ffd9c4; }
  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  a:hover { color: var(--accent-2); }
  :focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 3px; }

  .shell { display: flex; }
  .rail {
    position: sticky; top: 0; align-self: flex-start; width: var(--rail-w); height: 100vh; flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 34px 26px; border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; background: var(--bg); z-index: 20;
  }
  .rail-brand { padding-bottom: 6px; }
  .rail-brand .nm { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); }
  .rail-brand b { font-family: var(--head); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; font-size: 18px; display: block; margin-top: 4px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .rail-brand i { color: var(--muted); font-style: normal; font-size: 12.5px; display: block; margin-top: 2px; }
  .rail-list { list-style: none; margin-top: 22px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1px; overflow-y: auto; }
  .rail-item {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 11px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--muted);
    padding: 7px 11px; border-radius: 6px; cursor: pointer; transition: color .15s, background .15s;
    border-left: 2px solid transparent; width: 100%; text-align: left; background: none;
  }
  .rail-item:hover { color: var(--ink); background: var(--alt); }
  .rail-item .rn { color: var(--faint); font-size: 11px; width: 20px; flex-shrink: 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .rail-item.active { color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-tint); border-left-color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .rail-item.active .rn { color: var(--accent); }
  .rail-div { margin-top: 16px; margin-bottom: 4px; padding: 0 11px; font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.13em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); font-weight: 600; }
  .rail-foot { margin-top: auto; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.9; padding-top: 16px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }

  main { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .panel { padding: 74px clamp(24px, 6vw, 100px); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); position: relative; }
  .panel.alt { background: var(--alt); }
  .panel-inner { width: 100%; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }

  .eyebrow { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }
  .eyebrow .sec { color: var(--faint); }

  h1 { font-family: var(--head); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.01em; font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 3.7rem); line-height: 1.05; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.03em; color: var(--ink); text-wrap: balance; }
  h2 { font-family: var(--head); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); line-height: 1.12; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.024em; color: var(--ink); text-wrap: balance; }
  h3 { font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 650; color: var(--ink); letter-spacing: -0.008em; }
  .lede { font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.6vw, 1.22rem); color: var(--muted); max-width: 64ch; margin-top: 18px; line-height: 1.6; }
  .lede strong, p.body strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  p.body { color: var(--text); max-width: 66ch; margin-top: 16px; }
  em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }
  code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.84em; background: var(--alt); border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 4px; color: var(--accent); }

  .reveal { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(16px); transition: opacity .6s ease, transform .6s ease; }
  .reveal.in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }

  /* hero */
  #hero { padding-top: 90px; padding-bottom: 84px; }
  .hero-kicker { font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 22px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .hero-kicker .live { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; color: var(--green); }
  .hero-kicker .live::before { content: ""; width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--green); }
  .hero-sub { font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.4rem); color: var(--muted); max-width: 60ch; margin-top: 22px; line-height: 1.55; }
  .hero-sub strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .hero-cta { margin-top: 34px; display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; }
  .btn { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; padding: 11px 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; transition: all .15s; background: var(--card); display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; }
  .btn.primary { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: #fff; }
  .btn.primary:hover { background: var(--accent-2); border-color: var(--accent-2); }
  .btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .btn.primary:hover { color: #fff; }

  /* executive summary strip */
  .exec { margin-top: 44px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 0; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .exec .cell { padding: 20px 22px; border-right: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }
  .exec .cell:last-child { border-right: none; }
  .exec .cell .n { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); letter-spacing: -0.02em; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .exec .cell .l { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 5px; line-height: 1.4; }

  /* stack table */
  .spec { margin-top: 28px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .spec .row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr; gap: 18px; padding: 14px 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); font-size: 0.96rem; }
  .spec .row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .spec .row:nth-child(even) { background: var(--card); }
  .spec .k { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .spec .v { color: var(--text); }
  .spec .v b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .honest { margin-top: 18px; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--text); background: var(--amber-tint); border: 1px solid #eeddb8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; }
  .honest b { color: var(--amber-ink); }

  /* decision: problem + contrast + principle */
  .problem { margin-top: 22px; font-size: 1.06rem; color: var(--ink); background: var(--accent-tint); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 22px; max-width: 70ch; }
  .problem .pl { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .contrast { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 24px; }
  .side { border-radius: 12px; padding: 22px; border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--card); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .side.naive { border-top: 3px solid var(--red); }
  .side.built { border-top: 3px solid var(--green); }
  .side .st { font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 13px; }
  .side.naive .st { color: var(--red); } .side.built .st { color: var(--green); }
  .side p { color: var(--text); font-size: 0.97rem; } .side p + p { margin-top: 11px; }
  .side p b { color: var(--ink); }
  .principle { margin-top: 24px; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 4px 0 4px 22px; font-weight: 500; }
  .principle .wl { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; }

  /* feature cards */
  .feat-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 18px; margin-top: 26px; }
  .feat { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 11px; padding: 20px; box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .feat h3 { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .feat p { color: var(--text); font-size: 0.93rem; }

  /* metric tiles */
  .metrics { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px,1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-top: 26px; }
  .metric { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 11px; padding: 18px 20px; box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .metric .mv { font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .metric .mv.green { color: var(--green); }
  .metric .ml { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 5px; }

  /* scale roadmap */
  .road { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 28px; }
  .road .col { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; padding: 22px; background: var(--card); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .road .cl { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .road .breaks .cl { color: var(--red); } .road .target .cl { color: var(--accent); }
  .road ul { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  .road li { font-size: 0.96rem; color: var(--text); padding-left: 22px; position: relative; }
  .road li::before { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 1px; font-weight: 700; }
  .road .breaks li::before { content: "—"; color: var(--red); }
  .road .target li::before { content: "→"; color: var(--accent); }
  .road li b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .road li .note { color: var(--muted); }
  .carry { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; margin-top: 20px; }
  .carry div { font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.65; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; color: var(--text); background: var(--alt); }
  .carry .cf b { color: var(--green); } .carry .rd b { color: var(--amber-ink); }

  /* summary */
  .quote { margin-top: 24px; font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text); max-width: 70ch; }
  .quote strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .oneline { margin-top: 26px; background: var(--accent-tint); border-radius: 12px; padding: 22px 24px; font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink); max-width: 70ch; }
  .oneline .ql { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); display: block; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .oneline i { font-style: italic; }
  .close-links { display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-top: 30px; flex-wrap: wrap; }

  .progress { position: fixed; top: 0; left: var(--rail-w); right: 0; height: 3px; background: var(--line-soft); z-index: 40; }
  .progress i { display: block; height: 100%; width: 0%; background: var(--accent); transition: width .1s linear; }

  @media (max-width: 920px) {
    .rail { display: none; } .progress { left: 0; } main { width: 100%; }
    .contrast, .road, .carry { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .exec { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } .exec .cell:nth-child(2) { border-right: none; }
    .spec .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
    .panel { padding: 56px 22px; } body { font-size: 16px; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { animation-duration: .001ms !important; transition-duration: .001ms !important; } html { scroll-behavior: auto; } .reveal { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
</style>
</head>
<body>


<div class="progress"><i id="progBar"></i></div>

<div class="shell">
  <nav class="rail" aria-label="Contents">
    <div class="rail-brand"><span class="nm">Austin Nafe</span><b>TM Go</b><i>Architecture case study</i></div>
    <ul class="rail-list" id="railList">
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="hero"><span class="rn">00</span> Overview</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="diagram"><span class="rn">◆</span> Interactive diagram</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="stack"><span class="rn">01</span> System &amp; stack</button></li>
      <li class="rail-div">Key engineering decisions</li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d1"><span class="rn">01</span> Agent design</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d2"><span class="rn">02</span> State management</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d3"><span class="rn">03</span> Inbox modeling</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d4"><span class="rn">04</span> Reliability</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d5"><span class="rn">05</span> Evaluation</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d6"><span class="rn">06</span> Data model</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="d7"><span class="rn">07</span> Delivery</button></li>
      <li style="margin-top:10px;padding:2px 9px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;letter-spacing:0.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent)">Depth</li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="intel"><span class="rn">08</span> Where the intelligence lives</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="breaks"><span class="rn">09</span> Failure modes &amp; options</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="cost"><span class="rn">10</span> Token economics</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="stateng"><span class="rn">11</span> The state engine</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="modelbreaks"><span class="rn">12</span> When the model breaks</button></li>
      <li class="rail-div">Forward</li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="scale"><span class="rn">02</span> Scaling path</button></li>
      <li><button class="rail-item" data-goto="talk"><span class="rn">03</span> Summary</button></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="rail-foot"><a href="/">← Back to portfolio</a><br><a href="/intelligence">Austin's Intelligence</a><br><a href="/RESUME_2026.pdf">Résumé (PDF)</a></div>
  </nav>

  <main>
    <!-- OVERVIEW -->
    <section class="panel" id="hero" data-rung="hero">
      <div class="panel-inner">
        <div class="hero-kicker">
          <span>Architecture Case Study</span>
          <span class="live">Live in production</span>
        </div>
        <h1>TM Go</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">An autonomous tour-management agent for independent bands — it advances shows over email and SMS, reads free-form venue replies, extracts the logistics, and keeps each tour's records in sync. This document covers the system, the <strong>seven engineering decisions</strong> that made it safe to write to a band's real data, and how it scales.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn primary" href="https://toursync-deploy.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the live application</a>
          <button class="btn" data-goto="stack">System overview</button>
          <a class="btn" href="/RESUME_2026.pdf">Résumé</a>
        </div>
        <div class="exec">
          <div class="cell"><div class="n">LangGraph</div><div class="l">Bounded state-machine agent, not an open loop</div></div>
          <div class="cell"><div class="n">32</div><div class="l">Adversarial email-chain cases; 100% on both providers</div></div>
          <div class="cell"><div class="n">2</div><div class="l">LLM providers with automatic failover</div></div>
          <div class="cell"><div class="n">59</div><div class="l">API routes; Pydantic-validated, containerized</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- INTERACTIVE DIAGRAM -->
    <section class="panel" id="diagram" data-rung="diagram">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Overview · 01</span> Interactive architecture</div>
        <h2>See it two ways — the loop today, and the refactor</h2>
        <p class="lede">Toggle between <strong>how it works now</strong> (the ReAct loop and its ~25-tool belt) and <strong>the refactor</strong> (a bounded node graph). The graph is live — click any node for what it does; the amber trail animates the real "advance a show" path.</p>
        <div style="border:1px solid var(--line,#e5e2dc);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;background:#0b0f14;box-shadow:0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.18)">
          <iframe src="/tmgo-architecture.html" title="TM Go interactive architecture"
                  style="width:100%;height:860px;border:0;display:block" loading="lazy"></iframe>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)">
          <a href="/tmgo-architecture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the diagram full-screen →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STACK -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="stack" data-rung="stack">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">01</span> System &amp; stack</div>
        <h2>System overview</h2>
        <p class="lede">The real deployed system, with prototype shortcuts named as such rather than hidden.</p>
        <div class="spec">
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Frontend</span><span class="v"><b>React + Vite</b>, deployed on Vercel</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">API</span><span class="v"><b>Starlette → FastAPI</b> — 59 routes; Pydantic-validated AI endpoints with OpenAPI docs</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Agent core</span><span class="v"><b>LangGraph StateGraph</b> — a router dispatching to explicit, deterministic pipelines</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">LLM</span><span class="v">LangChain <code>init_chat_model</code> — OpenAI primary, <b>Anthropic failover</b></span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">SMS</span><span class="v">FastAPI + <b>Twilio</b> webhook</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Data</span><span class="v"><b>Google Sheets</b> (per-band system-of-record) + <b>Supabase Postgres</b> (auth, storage, AI telemetry — and now the <b>migration target</b>: venues + email state dual-written behind a parity gate, cutover on <code>TMGO_STORE=db</code>)</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Scheduler</span><span class="v"><b>Railway</b> cron worker for reminders</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Reliability</span><span class="v"><code>traced_llm</code> observability + closed-loop evaluation + <code>/api/ai-health</code> dashboard</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Delivery</span><span class="v"><b>GitHub Actions</b> CI (build · lint · eval gate) + Docker; Vercel/Render auto-deploy</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="honest"><b>Deliberately not included:</b> billing (no Stripe yet). The API was Starlette, upgraded to FastAPI specifically for the AI endpoints. Naming what isn't there is part of the point.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D1 -->
    <section class="panel" id="d1" data-rung="d1">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 01</span> Agent design</div>
        <h2>A bounded state-machine agent</h2>
        <div class="problem"><span class="pl">The problem</span>Advancing a show is a multi-step, correctness-sensitive workflow. Writing an incorrect load-in time into a band's real tour data is worse than writing nothing at all — so the agent's behavior has to be predictable.</div>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">Naive approach</div>
            <p>A <b>ReAct agent</b> that reasons and picks tools freely each turn.</p>
            <p>It re-plans every turn, its tool order is non-deterministic, and it can skip steps. Effective in a demo; unacceptable for a workflow that writes to real data.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st">What I shipped</div>
            <p>A hand-authored <b>LangGraph StateGraph</b>. A router node classifies intent once, then dispatches to explicit pipelines (e.g. <code>advance</code>: read venue → draft email).</p>
            <p>The LLM only drafts prose and parses free text into JSON. Routing, sheet I/O, validation, and email transport are <b>deterministic Python</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Principle</span>Determinism where correctness matters; the model only where language matters. The right move was not a more capable agent — it was a more constrained one.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D2 -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="d2" data-rung="d2">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 02</span> State management</div>
        <h2>Deriving state rather than storing it</h2>
        <div class="problem"><span class="pl">The problem</span>The interface must always show the correct "whose turn is it" across dozens of concurrent venue threads.</div>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">Naive approach</div>
            <p>A stored <code>status</code> column on each thread.</p>
            <p>It drifts out of sync: a reply arrives but the column still reads <code>sent</code>, so the interface reports the wrong state with full confidence.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st">What I shipped</div>
            <p>"Whose turn" is computed as a <b>pure function</b> of <code>(messages, sheet facts)</code> — derived, never stored.</p>
            <p>A derived value cannot drift from the source of truth. A stored copy eventually always does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Principle</span>If a value can be recomputed from the source of truth, deriving it removes an entire class of consistency bugs.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D3 -->
    <section class="panel" id="d3" data-rung="d3">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 03</span> Inbox modeling</div>
        <h2>The inbox as a workflow queue</h2>
        <div class="problem"><span class="pl">The problem</span>Send outreach, receive replies, convert replies into structured facts, and generate the next email — across dozens of venue threads at once.</div>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">Naive approach</div>
            <p>Build an email client. That merges three representations of one conversation: <b>live IMAP, logged sends, and optimistic UI state</b>.</p>
            <p>They disagree — messages reorder, duplicate, and deleted ones reappear.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st">What I shipped</div>
            <p>Reframed as a <b>queue keyed on the show</b>, not the message. Each cycle reconstructs "what to ask" from <code>(questions enabled) − (facts on sheet)</code>.</p>
            <p>The system never relies on remembered conversation state — it recomputes what remains outstanding.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Principle</span>Collapsing three sources of truth into one derived view eliminated the entire ordering-and-duplication bug class.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D4 -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="d4" data-rung="d4">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 04</span> Reliability</div>
        <h2>Reliability as a design requirement</h2>
        <p class="lede">Each of these exists because a specific failure would place an incorrect fact in front of a band on tour. They are requirements, not polish.</p>
        <div class="feat-grid">
          <div class="feat"><h3>Provider failover</h3><p>OpenAI primary, <b>Anthropic on failure</b>. A single provider's outage is not the product's outage.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Tolerant parsing</h3><p>JSON extraction survives code fences and stray prose, so a formatting quirk doesn't fail a valid extraction.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Human-in-the-loop</h3><p>Extractions are reviewed before reaching the sheet. Gap-fill only — a confirmed value is never overwritten.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Validated inputs</h3><p>A malformed request returns a clean <code>422</code>, not a deep <code>KeyError</code> several calls into the stack.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D5 -->
    <section class="panel" id="d5" data-rung="d5">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 05</span> Evaluation &amp; observability</div>
        <h2>Measuring correctness, and learning from corrections</h2>
        <div class="feat-grid">
          <div class="feat"><h3>Online — closed loop</h3><p>The human review step logs every accept, edit, and reject. Each correction is a labeled failure — a live dataset of the agent's weak points at <b>zero labeling cost</b>.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Offline — CI gate</h3><p>A golden-set harness scores field extraction and <b>fails the build below 80%</b>. Now <b>32 email-chain cases</b>, 10 with <b>negative controls</b> — invented answers cost score, so abstaining beats guessing. Reruns <b>weekly on a schedule</b>, since model drift doesn't wait for a commit.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>One tracing seam</h3><p><code>traced_llm</code> is the single point every model call passes through — recording endpoint, model, latency, tokens, estimated cost, and whether failover fired.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="metrics">
          <div class="metric"><div class="mv green">100%</div><div class="ml">Live extraction accuracy</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="mv">p50 / p95</div><div class="ml">Latency, tracked live</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="mv">Cost / call</div><div class="ml">Per-request spend</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="mv">Failover %</div><div class="ml">Provider health</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">In one sentence</span>The agent is instrumented end to end — per-call cost, latency, and failures are visible live, and every human correction becomes labeled evaluation data that gates deployment.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D6 -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="d6" data-rung="d6">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 06</span> Data model</div>
        <h2>A prototype data model with a defined ceiling</h2>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st" style="color:var(--accent)">Why it was the right call</div>
            <p>Google Sheets is each band's <b>system-of-record</b>. Bands can see their own data with <b>zero setup</b> — ideal for proving the workflow.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">Where it breaks</div>
            <p>It is also what breaks first at scale: <b>API quotas, no transactions, no concurrency guarantees</b>.</p>
            <p>I named that ceiling before hitting it — and the exit is now underway: a staged, parity-gated migration to Postgres (see <b>Scaling path</b>).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Principle</span>Selecting the right tool for the current stage — and stating exactly where it will fail before it does — is the engineering judgment, not a gap in it.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- D7 -->
    <section class="panel" id="d7" data-rung="d7">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Decision 07</span> Delivery</div>
        <h2>Evaluation-gated continuous delivery</h2>
        <div class="feat-grid">
          <div class="feat"><h3>CI on every push</h3><p>GitHub Actions runs the frontend build, a real-error lint, and the <b>evaluation gate</b>. A regression in extraction accuracy blocks the merge.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Automatic deploy</h3><p>A passing <code>main</code> deploys itself to Vercel and Render — no manual release step to forget.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Containerized backend</h3><p>Docker packages the backend to run identically anywhere. This container is the unit a worker pool would replicate at scale.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Why it matters</span>Because the backend is already containerized, moving to a worker pool is a deployment change, not a rewrite — the scaling path was designed in from the start.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SCALE -->
    <!-- INTEL -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="intel" data-rung="intel">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Depth · 01</span> Where the intelligence lives</div>
        <h2>Two LLM seams. Everything else is code — on purpose.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The model appears at exactly two points, both language problems: <b>parsing</b> a venue's free-form reply into typed fields, and <b>drafting</b> the outbound message. Routing, scheduling, sheet writes, validation, retries — deterministic Python. That placement is the load-bearing decision the other six hang off.</p>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">What this costs me</div>
            <p><b>Flexibility.</b> A new capability means writing a pipeline, not prompting the agent to improvise one. The system cannot handle a workflow nobody designed. That's a real limitation and I'd say it plainly.</p>
            <p>If the product needed open-ended exploration — "figure out this venue's whole booking process" — this shape would be wrong, and I'd add a <b>bounded free node</b>: one constrained agentic step with a budget, timeout, and typed output, inside the deterministic graph.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st">What it buys</div>
            <p><b>Verifiability.</b> Every path is enumerable, so every path is testable — that's what makes the 80% CI gate and the closed feedback loop possible at all. You can't regression-gate an agent whose control flow is different every run.</p>
            <p><b>Blast-radius control.</b> The model's worst output is a bad draft or a bad parse — both pass through a human gate before touching real data. It structurally cannot send, write, or delete.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Tied together</span>All seven decisions are one bet made seven times: <b>trade flexibility for verifiability wherever the system touches real data</b>. Derived state, the queue view, the eval gate — each is that same trade in a different subsystem. They don't contradict; they compound.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BREAKS -->
    <section class="panel" id="breaks" data-rung="breaks">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Depth · 02</span> Failure modes &amp; options</div>
        <h2>Where it breaks, where it doesn't — and the options when it does</h2>
        <p class="lede">A systems answer, not a feature list: each subsystem's break condition, observed or anticipated, with the mitigation shipped today and the options in reserve.</p>
        <div class="spec">
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Extraction</span><span class="v"><b>Breaks when:</b> a venue writes something genuinely ambiguous ("load in around when doors-ish"). <b>Today:</b> human review catches it; the correction becomes labeled eval data — and the specific ways the model misreads email now have their own adversarial suite (see <b>When the model breaks</b>). <b>Options:</b> per-field confidence with abstention, dual-model consensus on low-confidence fields, targeted golden-set additions from the failure log.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Sheets I/O</span><span class="v"><b>Breaks when:</b> API quota bursts or two writers collide — no transactions. <b>Today:</b> the real fix is <i>in flight</i> — venue facts and email state <b>dual-write to Postgres</b>, a parity endpoint proves the stores agree, and reads cut over behind <code>TMGO_STORE=db</code>; a Sheets export endpoint starts the demotion from database to report. <b>Left:</b> remaining domains, then the contract step.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Email ingest</span><span class="v"><b>Breaks when:</b> IMAP polling lags or a cold start misses the window. <b>Today:</b> the derived queue means a late-seen message just resolves on the next cycle — nothing is lost, only delayed. And the event-driven path is half-built: a <b>Gmail push receiver</b> acks fast and hands the work to the job queue, off the request path. <b>Left:</b> GCP pub/sub + watch enablement.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">LLM providers</span><span class="v"><b>Breaks when:</b> OpenAI degrades. <b>Today:</b> automatic Anthropic failover, recorded per call by <code>traced_llm</code>. <b>Both down:</b> the graph degrades to queue-and-notify — drafts wait, nothing silently drops. Deterministic paths (reminders, sheet sync) keep running.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">State</span><span class="v"><b>Where it doesn't break:</b> turn-status and the work queue are <i>derived</i>, so they cannot desync from messages + sheet. This bug class is closed by construction, not patched.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Writes</span><span class="v"><b>Where it doesn't break:</b> nothing the model produces reaches a band's data without an approval click. The failure mode "AI corrupted my tour sheet" has no path through the graph.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Quality drift</span><span class="v"><b>Breaks when:</b> a prompt or model change quietly degrades extraction. <b>Today:</b> it can't ship — the CI eval gate blocks below 80%. <b>Options:</b> multi-trial consistency (pass^k) and paraphrase-perturbation cases to catch instability a single pass hides.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="honest"><b>The honest one:</b> the current single-run eval measures capability, not reliability. An agent can pass once and still be flaky. The upgrade path is multi-trial pass^k with perturbations — capability says it <i>can</i> work; consistency says it <i>keeps</i> working.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- COST -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="cost" data-rung="cost">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Depth · 03</span> Token economics</div>
        <h2>Cost as a designed number, not a surprise</h2>
        <p class="lede">One advance cycle touches the model exactly twice — parse the reply, draft the response — so cost scales with <b>shows advanced</b>, not with chat length or agent wandering. That's a consequence of the bounded graph: no re-planning loops means no unbounded token spend. The levers, in the order I'd pull them:</p>
        <div class="spec">
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Metering first</span><span class="v">You can't optimize what you don't measure — <code>traced_llm</code> already records tokens and cost per call, per provider. Every lever below is verified against that meter, not vibes.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Model tiering <em>(shipped)</em></span><span class="v">Different jobs run different tiers, chosen by measurement: quick classification and summaries run <code>gpt-4o-mini</code>; field <b>extraction runs gpt-4o</b>, because grounding errors there reach real tour data and the eval gate showed the smaller model wasn't worth the savings. Tiering isn't "small everywhere" — it's the meter deciding where quality pays.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Response caching <em>(shipped)</em></span><span class="v">The gateway caches <b>deterministic calls only</b> — temperature-0 routing, parsing, extraction. Same prompt inside the TTL → same answer, zero tokens. Drafting runs warm and is <i>never</i> cached: a fresh draft should read fresh. A cache that can only serve calls whose output is a pure function of input never adds a failure mode.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Context budget <em>(next lever)</em></span><span class="v">A recency window plus hard caps on history. Without a budget, every turn's cost grows with transcript length — the quiet cost leak in most chat products.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">Per-tenant budgets <em>(shipped)</em></span><span class="v">The LLM gateway (the <code>traced_llm</code> seam grown up) enforces a per-band daily token ceiling <b>before</b> the call and records usage after — one runaway tour can't eat the month. Design detail: a blown budget deliberately does <i>not</i> trigger provider failover — both providers cost tokens; the answer is "not today," not "try the other model."</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Principle</span>Strategic cost design is architecture, not discounts: bound the loop, tier the models, cache the constants, budget the context — and meter everything so each lever's payoff is a measured number.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STATE ENGINE -->
    <section class="panel" id="stateng" data-rung="stateng">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Depth · 04</span> The state engine</div>
        <h2>Records, events, projections — why the views can't disagree</h2>
        <p class="lede">Decisions 2 and 3 generalized into the engine that now runs the inbox. The root cause of every state bug we hit — a count saying 1 while the list said 0, a deleted venue leaving a ghost, old emails re-tagging a re-uploaded venue — was the same: <b>the same truth derived in more than one place.</b> The fix is a model, not a patch.</p>
        <div class="spec">
          <div class="row"><span class="k">① Records</span><span class="v">The facts — a venue's row. The source of truth (Sheets today, Postgres later).</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">② Events</span><span class="v">An append-only, timestamped log of things that <i>happened</i> — sent, received, accepted, edited, <b>deleted (a tombstone, not erasure)</b> — each with an idempotency key.</span></div>
          <div class="row"><span class="k">③ Projections</span><span class="v">Every "state" the UI shows — work queue, advancing status, the reply badge — is a <b>pure function</b> of records + events. Computed in exactly one place; chat and buttons read the same projection, so they <i>cannot</i> disagree.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="contrast">
          <div class="side naive">
            <div class="st">The bug this killed (shipped fix)</div>
            <p>Delete a venue, re-upload it — and old emails re-tagged the new one. Two matchers in two endpoints, no time bound.</p>
            <p><b>Fix, by construction:</b> one shared matcher (<code>_match_email_to_venue</code>), a <code>watch_since</code> stamp at venue creation (<code>_stamp_venue_watch</code>), and a time-bound (<code>_watch_allows</code>): a reply counts only if it postdates the venue. A re-upload is a new aggregate with a fresh cutoff — prior events can't resurface.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="side built">
            <div class="st">The invariants (each maps to a bug it prevents)</div>
            <p>1 · one reducer per projection &nbsp;2 · idempotency keys (<code>message_id</code>, content-hash) &nbsp;3 · time-bounded projections &nbsp;4 · tombstone deletes &nbsp;5 · single-writer per aggregate + optimistic concurrency &nbsp;6 · read-your-writes for the actor, eventual for pollers.</p>
            <p><b>Every mutation — chat, button, inbound email — goes through one gated <code>apply(event)</code></b>: idempotent, atomic (best-effort on Sheets; a transaction on Postgres), and it emits which projections it invalidated — cache invalidation is an output of the write, not a cron job hoping to catch up.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">Cost tie-in</span>Derive-on-read can't drift but recomputes every turn; <b>materialized projections</b> buy the compute back without giving up correctness — cached under a version key, refreshed only on write-driven invalidation. Correctness first, then cost, in that order.</div>
        <p class="body" style="margin-top:16px"><b>Why it's a platform:</b> the engine is vertical-agnostic. A health vertical (Meridian) keeps the three layers, the funnel, and the invariants unchanged — it raises the gate policy, swaps Sheets for Postgres, and the event log doubles as the <b>compliance audit trail</b> for free. Same engine, harder rules — that's the generalization test.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHEN THE MODEL BREAKS -->
    <section class="panel alt" id="modelbreaks" data-rung="modelbreaks">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Depth · 05</span> When the model breaks</div>
        <h2>The model is wrong sometimes. That's a design input, not a bug report.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Reading a venue's email is the one place TM GO <i>must</i> use a model — free-form human text has no parser. So the question isn't whether the model errs; it's <b>which specific way</b>, and what catches each one. Every mitigation below is either code that runs before the model, a scoring rule that punishes the failure, or a gate the model can't cross. All of it is <b>tested</b>: 50 unit tests across six suites, plus a golden email-chain set of <b>32 extraction cases</b> — 10 of them carrying <b>negative controls</b>.</p>

        <div class="spec">
          <div class="row"><span class="k">It hallucinates a field</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> nobody mentioned parking, but a plausible parking answer appears anyway — the worst kind, because a confident wrong fact reads exactly like a right one. <b>Caught by:</b> the eval scorer counts <i>invented</i> answers against the score, not just missed ones — 10 cases ship <code>forbid</code> fields that must come back empty. A run that fills every field scores <i>worse</i> than one that abstains correctly. <b>Held by:</b> rule 8 — <i>never invent; omit any field the venue didn't address</i> — and the human review gate before anything reaches the sheet.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">It answers for the wrong show</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> a promoter replies about two dates in one email — "Oct 16 at Bowery doors are 8; your Nov 2 date at Music Hall, doors 7." A naive extractor grabs the last number it saw and writes 7 to the Bowery show. <b>Caught by:</b> a dedicated adversarial case (<i>multi-show bleed</i>) that fails if <i>any</i> other venue's numbers come back. This one is a real category — venue staff genuinely batch their replies.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">It's not a human at all</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> an auto-reply or a bounce gets read as content, and "I'm out of office until the 14th" becomes a load-in time. <b>Caught by: code, not the model.</b> <code>_deterministic_reply_kind()</code> matches machine signatures — <code>mailer-daemon</code>, <code>550 5.1.1</code>, "delivery status notification", "automatic reply" — and <b>short-circuits before any LLM call</b>. Cheaper, exact, and it removes an entire hallucination surface rather than prompting against it. The model never sees the message.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">It misreads severity</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> "we have to move your date" gets parsed for fields and quietly updates the sheet, while the actual message — <i>the show is in jeopardy</i> — never surfaces. <b>Caught by:</b> the model classifies every reply as one of five kinds (answer · redirect · question · <b>problem</b> · other), and <b>a <code>problem</code> classification is forbidden from producing field updates at all</b>. Cancellations, postponements, double-bookings and payment disputes route to a human by construction. Note the direction: the model is trusted to <i>raise</i> an alarm, never to <i>act</i> on one.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">It takes a "no" for silence</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> "no catering, sorry" gets dropped as a non-answer, so the system keeps asking — and the band looks like it isn't listening. <b>Caught by:</b> rule 4 (an explicit NO <i>is</i> an answer) with golden cases including the nastiest phrasing: <i>"I wish we had catering"</i> — still a no. Rule 5 covers the same trap for conditional answers ("parking only if you arrive before 5" is an answer, qualifier and all).</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">The provider degrades</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> quality quietly drops with a model change, or the primary provider goes down and the failover behaves differently. <b>Caught by:</b> the golden set runs against <b>both providers</b> — the last full run scored <b>100% on gpt-4o-mini and on claude-haiku</b>, so failover is a measured equivalence rather than a hope. The suite reruns <b>on a schedule</b> (Mondays), not just on push, because model drift doesn't wait for a commit.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">The pipe breaks, not the model</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> the least glamorous and most common — IMAP goes quiet, the server sends BYE, or a handler throws and silently kills the watch loop, so replies simply stop arriving and nothing looks broken. <b>Caught by:</b> 8 watcher tests that pin the distinctions that actually bite — a quiet timeout is <i>not</i> mail, keepalives are ignored until a real <code>EXISTS</code>, a refused IDLE raises <i>unsupported</i> rather than looking idle forever, backoff doubles and caps, and <b>an error in an event handler never kills the watch</b>.</span></div>

          <div class="row"><span class="k">Identity confusion</span><span class="v"><b>The failure:</b> the right extraction attached to the wrong venue — the state-engine bug class from the previous section, but arriving through the model's door. <b>Caught by:</b> 8 tagging tests holding the precedence rules explicit: an <b>explicit binding wins over everything</b>, the <code>watch_since</code> stamp blocks pre-existing mail from a re-uploaded venue, a not-yet-started venue collects nothing, unrelated mail stays untagged, and <b>fuzzy matching only runs when explicitly asked for</b> — the loosest matcher is opt-in, never a silent default.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">The pattern</span>Read the column down and one shape repeats: <b>the model proposes; code disposes.</b> Machine mail is classified before the model runs, invented answers cost score, a "problem" verdict is structurally barred from writing, and the loosest matcher requires explicit consent. None of that makes the model more accurate — it makes the model's mistakes <b>cheap</b>, which is the only property that survives contact with production.</div>

        <div class="honest"><b>Where this is still thin:</b> the extraction golden set is 32 cases and every rule in the prompt was <i>earned</i> — each maps to a specific case or a live miss that got promoted — but it's still a single-pass score. Capability, not consistency. The upgrade is the same one I named earlier and have since built elsewhere: multi-trial <code>pass^k</code> with paraphrase perturbations, so an answer that's right four times out of five is reported as flaky instead of passing. And the generation side (the drafted email) has only <b>2</b> judge-scored cases against the extraction side's 32 — the thinnest part of the harness, and the next thing I'd grow.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel" id="scale" data-rung="scale">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="sec">Forward · 02</span> Scaling path</div>
        <h2>The scaling path — no longer just a plan</h2>
        <p class="lede">This section used to say <i>"presented as a roadmap, not a current claim."</i> Most of it has since <strong>shipped</strong> — behind flags, fail-open, with the migration gated by measurement. Statuses below are per target, honestly marked.</p>
        <div class="road">
          <div class="col breaks">
            <div class="cl">Breaks first at 100×</div>
            <ul>
              <li><b>Sheets as database</b> — quotas, no transactions</li>
              <li><b>IMAP polling</b> and cold starts</li>
              <li><b>Synchronous LLM calls</b> on the request path</li>
              <li>The <b>three-source inbox merge</b></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="col target">
            <div class="cl">Target architecture — status</div>
            <ul>
              <li><b>Postgres system-of-record</b> <span class="note">— shipped for venues + email state: shadow dual-write, backfill, a parity gate, reads cut over on <code>TMGO_STORE=db</code>; Sheets demoting to an export. Remaining domains still to migrate.</span></li>
              <li><b>Queue + worker pool</b> <span class="note">— shipped: a Postgres-backed jobs table; workers claim via an atomic conditional update, so a racing pool has <b>exactly one winner — there's a test for the race</b>. Exponential backoff, dead-letter rows (never silently dropped). Runs in-process today (<code>TMGO_WORKERS</code>); promotes to a dedicated worker dyno with zero code change.</span></li>
              <li><b>LLM gateway</b> <span class="note">— shipped on the one <code>traced_llm</code> seam: deterministic-call caching, per-tenant daily budgets, a usage endpoint. Failover was already there.</span></li>
              <li><b>Event-driven email</b> <span class="note">— partial: the Gmail push receiver is live and feeds the worker queue (ack fast, process async); pub/sub + watch enablement remain.</span></li>
              <li><b>Multi-tenant isolation</b> <span class="note">— row-level security enabled on all five Postgres tables; the eval gate runs in CI plus a weekly scheduled pass.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle"><span class="wl">The migration pattern</span>Strangler fig, gated by measurement: <b>shadow dual-write → backfill → parity endpoint proves the stores agree → flip the read flag → demote the old store to an export.</b> Every step is reversible and the whole thing is flag-gated and fail-open — deploying it changes nothing until a switch flips. Cutover is earned by a parity number, not asserted.</div>
        <div class="carry">
          <div class="cf"><b>Carry forward:</b> deterministic-first design, provider failover, human-in-the-loop review, and the observability seam — the gateway and the queue were both built <i>on</i> existing seams, which is the proof the seams were real.</div>
          <div class="rd"><b>Still to do:</b> finish the store migration's remaining domains, enable pub/sub for push, and promote the worker pool out of process under real multi-tenant load. Named so the shipped column stays honest.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
